WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:04.360
The video game industry has never been more
innovative or exciting.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.480
But with so many great games coming out, it's
inevitable that some games will just slip

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:09.480
through the cracks.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.330
Here's a look at some incredible video games
no one bought.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.730
Vampire: The Masquerade—Bloodlines

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.850
Hardly anyone bought Vampire: The Masquerade
- Bloodlines when it came out over a decade

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:19.850
ago.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:23.580
Vampires weren’t yet sparkly, and the kind
of vampires Bloodlines dealt with were no

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.000
longer that popular.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.769
But those that did buy the game are not only
still playing it, but creating their own new

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:30.170
content for it.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:34.480
That's the kind of passion this hybrid RPG
inspires in its fans with its immersive story

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.160
of a vampire who must negotiate a world of
undead clans competing for supremacy.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.550
It remains a great treat for retro gamers,
but be careful: even now, the hotel level

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.290
is scary enough to cause nightmares, so play
at your own risk.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.930
Panzer Dragoon Saga

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.840
After two traditional rail shooters, the Panzer
Dragoon franchise took an unexpected detour

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.640
into the realm of Final Fantasy style RPGs
with this classic 1998 release.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.050
Panzer Dragoon Saga has been acclaimed as
one of the greatest RPGs of all time.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.310
So why didn't anyone buy it?

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:09.120
Well, it had the misfortune of being released
for the Sega Saturn in 1998 - the same year

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.610
the platform was discontinued in North America.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.440
Talk about bad timing!

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.580
Psi-Ops: The Mindgate Conspiracy

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.980
In this one-and-done adventure from 2004 for
the Xbox and PS2, players assume control of

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.390
an amnesiac with psychic powers and a nefarious
paramilitary group on his tail.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.430
It may not sound particularly innovative now,
but that's because dozens of games have since

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.150
copied its mind-bending telekinetic gameplay.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:36.760
Sadly, fans of the game never got a sequel,
and likely never will, as the studio behind

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.280
Psi-Ops closed in 2010.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.280
Deadly Premonition

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.790
Lots of games get mixed reviews, but Deadly
Premonition actually earned the Guinness World

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.400
Record as the Most Critically Polarizing Survival
Horror Game.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.170
Taking many cues from Twin Peaks and Japanese
horror, Deadly Premonition is a must-play

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.220
title for fans of B-movies and anyone wanting
a bizarre, left-field experience.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.470
You have to buy gas for your car; without
coffee, you'll get sleepy.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:06.420
On top of this, the graphics aren't great
even for 2010, and the combat system is terrible.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.420
In other words, it's not for everyone.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.840
But if you don't hate it, chances are you're
going to love it.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.700
With Deadly Premonition, there's really no
middle ground.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.750
Viking: Battle for Asgard

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.710
There's something to be said for a game of
mindless destruction spent mowing down enemies

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:20.710
like weeds.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:24.410
Sure, it might not break new ground, but if
you want to break some skulls instead, this

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.410
is your game.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.890
Lost in the shuffle among more hyped and more
innovative titles, Viking: Battle for Asgard

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.920
just does one simple thing - but it does it
very, very well indeed.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.160
The Saboteur

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.270
The final game from developer Pandemic Studios,
The Saboteur is an open-world game set during

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.420
World War II, sort of like Grand Theft Auto
in German-occupied France.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.960
With noir-inspired graphics and an unusual
story-based style that sees you inspiring

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.440
rebellion against the Nazi overlords, The
Saboteur feels about as far from cookie cutter

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.250
World War II shooters as possible.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.200
And that's a good thing.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.200
Vanquish

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.090
Run and gun, duck and cover, punch giants
in the face: It's not just the mantra of this

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.400
fast paced third person shooter, it's also
a motto for life.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.000
Vanquish doesn't necessarily do anything new,
but it does everything well, with the controls

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.680
and graphics all top notch for a last generation
actioner.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.760
Fans at the time thought it looked too derivative
of games like Bayonetta, but sometimes, fun

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.070
trumps innovation.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.540
This is one of those times.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.010
Brütal Legend

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:28.240
Designer Tim Schaefer previously brought gamers
idiosyncratic titles like Psychonauts and

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.430
Grim Fandlango.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.580
And this is no exception.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.760
A rock and roll love letter featuring the
likes of and Ozzy Osbourne and Lemmy Kilmister

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.040
from Motörhead among its voice cast, Brütal
Legend finds your lowly roadie player character

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.980
and their party roaming across a heavy metal
hellscape, securing territory through real-time

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.380
combat and strategy.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.890
The game, while a poor seller, was praised
for its wit and charm.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.040
Check it out - and rock on!

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.130
Singularity

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.490
Singularity is a first-person shooter with
a heady time manipulation mechanic that allows

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.120
you to travel forward and backward through
time, affecting gunplay and the convoluted

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.120
plot.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.490
Accused of being a BioShock knockoff when
it was released, this entertaining romp is

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.490
still worth a run-through for some memorable
moments and nightmarish monsters - and if

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.640
you can find some friends to join you for
multiplayer, even better.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:14.690
Thanks for watching!

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.239
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:19.590
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

